{
  "coreName": "Ruffle",
  "systemId": "Flash",
  "systemNames": {
    "Flash": "Flash"
  },
  "author": "Ruffle contributors (ruffle-rs/ruffle); chimera core by Sergio Martin",
  "url": "https://github.com/ruffle-rs/ruffle",
  "extensions": {
    ".swf": "Flash"
  },
  "romFile": "game",
  "deterministic": true,
  "memoryLayoutMiB": [
    1024,
    16,
    16,
    64,
    1024
  ],
  "_memoryLayoutMiB_note": "sbrk, sealed, invisible, plain, mmap. A Rust guest: std's allocator wants a real heap, and a movie's display list and decoded assets live on it. 1 GiB of each since 2026-10-23: Corporate Climber's heap reaches 315 MiB and its large blocks 320 MiB by frame 55848; with 256 and 512 the heap spilled into the large-block area, broke it into pieces, and a 12.8 MB picture found no room. Only the pages a movie touches cost anything.",
  "video": {
    "greenzoneSurvivesReload": true,
    "_comment_greenzone": "TRUE: a project's greenzone this core drew on a GPU may be loaded by a later session (docs/gpu-bridge.md in Chimera, 'Keeping the greenzone across sessions'). Measured 2026-09-16: the real 8916-frame nss102 project, its 626 MB greenzone saved by one process and loaded by another; frame 8915 drawn identically, with a control frame (8815) that differs. Until 2026-10-18 this was Ruffle's name on a list in Chimera.",
    "gpuStatesSurviveTheContext": true,
    "_comment_gpuStates": "Only ever asked on the opengl-hw renderer, which is the only one with a host context to lose: the renderer rebuilds its GL objects when the calls land on a context that is not the one they came from (the bridge's GL_OP_CONTEXT_ID), so a state this core makes opens again in a later session - its greenzone survives a reload instead of being discarded. On the software renderer the GL objects are guest memory and the savestate already carries them, so the core answers 'cannot tell' and nothing is ever rebuilt.",
    "_comment": "The stage, at scale 1: the live size comes from GetVideoWidth/Height per frame and is whatever the movie declares. Capacity here is the largest stage worth allowing. Drawn by ruffle's own wgpu renderer, on the Mesa softpipe compiled into the core (waterbox/gl-osmesa.cpp) or on the host's GL across the bridge (waterbox/gl-bridge.h) - see the renderer setting.",
    "width": 1920,
    "height": 1080,
    "getBgra": "GetVideoBgra",
    "vsyncNumerator": 60,
    "vsyncDenominator": 1,
    "_comment_vsync": "A fallback only: a Flash movie carries its own frame rate and the core reports it through GetVsyncNumerator/GetVsyncDenominator, again after any frame in which the movie changed it (stage.frameRate)."
  },
  "audio": {
    "_comment": "Ruffle's own software mixer, one frame at a time, 44.1 kHz stereo (the same backend shape ruffle's test harness uses). samplesPerFrame is CAPACITY: 44100/fps per frame, and a 1 fps movie is the worst case.",
    "sampleRate": 44100,
    "channels": 2,
    "samplesPerFrame": 44100,
    "get": "GetAudio"
  },
  "input": {
    "name": "Keyboard and Mouse",
    "_comment": "Mouse Left/Middle/Right, then the keyboard row by row as it sits on a desk. The two axes are the pointer in stage pixels. Buttons are levels; the core turns level changes into Ruffle's key/mouse events at the start of each frame. Wire order is generated by waterbox/input-table.py.",
